# Linux Security Lab

## Objective

The objective of this lab is to understand basic Linux security
concepts, commands, file permissions, users, and groups.

## Topics Covered

- Linux commands
- Users and groups
- File permissions
- chmod
- sudo
- File ownership
- System security
- Basic Linux troubleshooting

## What I Learned

I learned how Linux uses users, groups, and file permissions
to control access to files and system resources.

I also learned how commands such as chmod and sudo can be
used to manage permissions and perform administrative tasks.

## Linux Security Commands

Some of the commands I studied include:

- `ls`
- `pwd`
- `cd`
- `whoami`
- `id`
- `chmod`
- `chown`
- `sudo`

## Security Best Practices

- Use strong passwords
- Give users only the permissions they need
- Keep Linux systems updated
- Avoid using sudo unnecessarily
- Protect sensitive files
- Review file permissions regularly
- Use trusted software and repositories

## What This Lab Demonstrates

This lab demonstrates my understanding of basic Linux
security concepts and access control.

## Conclusion

This lab helped me understand how Linux manages users,
permissions, and access to system resources. These concepts
are important foundations for cybersecurity.
